"answers": [
"Cognitive psychology studies the mind by asking how information is represented and how it is changed. This is the information-processing perspective. Perception, attention, memory and reasoning can each be studied this way. A computational explanation also says what problem a system solves.",
"Literature, philosophy and astrology are other ways of trying to understand the mind. They can talk about the same mind and still work in different ways. Three questions separate the approaches. They are what each one tries to explain, how it tests whether a claim is true, and how its practitioners reach agreement.",
"Cognitive psychology tries to explain how people process information about their environment. It also tries to explain the mechanisms involved in perceiving, acting and thinking. Claims are tested with controlled laboratory experiments and with models. An experiment compares conditions. A simulation or statistical model turns an explanation into a prediction that can be compared with data. Publications and replications let other researchers check a claim. Agreement is often incomplete.",
"An intelligent robot would need to perceive and interpret its environment and select the relevant information. It would also need to learn, remember, communicate, plan, decide and act safely. Uses such as care, disaster response, logistics and assistance need several of these abilities at once. One correct identification is not enough.",
"Cognition includes perception, attention, remembering, telling apart the items in a category, visualizing, understanding and producing language, problem solving, reasoning and decision making. All of these include hidden processes that we may not be aware of. A response can feel immediate and still depend on several operations.",
"Cognitive psychology is the branch of psychology concerned with the scientific study of the mind. Everyday uses of the word “mind” point to different functions. Calling something to mind is remembering. Putting your mind to a problem is thinking. Making up your mind is deciding. “Sound mind” refers to mental functioning, and “a brilliant mind” refers to intellectual ability.",
"The mind creates and controls mental processes such as perception, attention, memory, emotions, language, deciding, thinking and reasoning. The mind also creates representations of the world so that we can act in it to reach our goals. A representation is information about something. A process uses or changes information. Some theories question whether processes and representations can always be separated.",
"Many philosophers wrote about the mind. Socrates had a distinct epistemology, which is an approach to knowledge. Laozi discussed the unreliability of perception. The scientific approach uses quantifiable, repeatable measurement. A claim has to be tied to observations that can be measured and made again."
],
