"answers": [
"Franciscus Donders (1868), a Dutch physiologist, ran one of the first cognitive psychology experiments. He wanted to measure how long it takes a person to make a decision. Reaction time is the interval between the presentation of a stimulus and the person’s response. The interval includes detecting the stimulus and making the response, as well as any decision.",
"In the simple reaction time task, the participant pushes a button as quickly as possible after a light appears. There is only one response. In the choice reaction time task, the participant pushes one button if the light is on the left and another if it is on the right. The choice task adds a decision about which response is correct.",
"The reaction time apparatus records the interval from the stimulus to the response. The equipment makes that interval measurable. The interval includes perceiving the light, any decision, and pressing the button. The apparatus records the total and does not show a thought directly.",
"In the simple task, the light flashes, the person perceives the light, and then presses the button. In the choice task, the left light flashes, the person perceives it and decides which button to push, and then presses the left button. The behavioral response can be recorded. The mental response in between is inferred.",
"Decision time equals choice reaction time minus simple reaction time. Choice reaction time was about 0.10 seconds longer than simple reaction time. Donders concluded that the decision took about 0.10 seconds, which is 100 milliseconds. The estimate assumes that the shared steps take about the same time in both tasks.",
"Donders (1868) showed that mental responses cannot be measured directly. They can be inferred from behavior. He measured reaction times and inferred how long the added mental step took. The subtraction approach is still used in neuroimaging. Researchers compare brain activity across conditions to estimate what an added operation contributes."
],
"remember": "Decision time equals choice reaction time minus simple reaction time, about 0.10 seconds in Donders (1868)."
