"answers": [
"John Watson (1878–1958) saw two problems with introspection. Results were extremely variable from person to person. Results were also difficult to verify, because they were explained by invisible inner processes. Watson proposed behaviorism. Behaviorism removes the mind as a topic of study and studies directly observable behavior. Reasoning, thinking and emotions were left out because they cannot be observed.",
"Watson’s behaviorist manifesto (1913) called psychology “a purely objective experimental branch of natural science.” The goal was to predict and control behavior without appealing to consciousness. Observable stimuli and responses were the basis for explanation.",
"Watson replaced the mind with observable behavior as the thing psychology studies. The question changed from “What does behavior tell us about the mind?” to “What is the relationship between environmental stimuli and behavior?” Both approaches can record the same action. They differ in what counts as an explanation of it.",
"Watson and Rayner (1920) studied the conditioning of fear in the Little Albert experiment. A nine-month-old child became frightened by a rat after a loud noise was paired with the rat. The rat did not cause this fear at first. After the pairings, the rat alone caused fear. This is classical conditioning, because the key relationship is between two stimuli. It is not operant conditioning, which works through the consequences of an action.",
"In classical conditioning, a neutral stimulus is paired with a stimulus that naturally produces a response. After repeated pairings, the neutral stimulus produces the response. Pavlov studied digestion. Conditioning came from his observation of anticipatory salivation, which is salivation before the food arrives.",
"B. F. Skinner (1940s–1960s) studied the relationships among situations, actions and consequences. In operant conditioning, consequences shape future behavior. Reinforcement makes a behavior more likely. Punishment makes a behavior less likely.",
"The early dates are Donders’ reaction time work in 1868, Wundt’s laboratory in 1879, Ebbinghaus’ forgetting curve in 1885 and James’ Principles of Psychology in 1890. Watson’s behaviorism is dated 1913, and Skinner’s operant conditioning is dated 1938."
],
"remember": "Watson replaced the mind with observable behavior. Little Albert is classical conditioning, and Skinner studied operant conditioning."
