"Perception is fast and effortless, so it feels automatic. The processing behind it is largely invisible to us. Roughly half the primate cortex is devoted to vision alone. Machine perception has repeatedly fallen short. Machines handled chess, proofs and algebra decades before reliable object recognition. The DARPA Urban Challenge (2007) showed how hard it is for robot vehicles to interpret a changing environment.",
"In 2007, machine vision relied on hand-written rules and on combining sensors. Vehicles finished a 60-mile urban course slowly, on a closed course. Now deep networks trained on millions of labeled images match or exceed human accuracy on benchmark object recognition. Four problems remain. They are novel viewpoints, occlusion, unusual lighting, and scenes with odd context.",
