"answers": [
"The inverse projection problem is the task of determining which object produced a particular image on the retina. We start with the retinal image and work outward to its source. One image could have come from infinitely many objects. A small object nearby and a huge object far away can produce identical retinal images.",
"Most objects in a normal scene are partly occluded, which means partly hidden by other objects. People still identify them from fragments. We also recognize objects that are blurred, poorly lit, or seen at the edge of vision. The visual system treats a partial view as a partial view, and not as a new shape. Perceptual completion means the visual system extends contours behind the object that hides them. A wheelbarrow behind a wall is still seen as one wheelbarrow.",
"Viewpoint invariance is the ability to recognize an object as the same object across changes in viewing angle. Every rotation produces a physically different retinal image. We have never seen most objects from most angles, and we recognize them anyway. This is difficult for machine vision and easy for young children."
],
"remember": "One retinal image fits many objects, most objects are partly hidden, and every viewpoint gives a different image."
