"answers": [
"There are two directions of processing. Information flows up from the receptors, which is bottom-up. Information flows down from what you already know, which is top-down. The incoming signal supplies sensory evidence. Knowledge, expectations and attention influence how that evidence is interpreted.",
"Bottom-up processing starts at the bottom of the system, where energy from the environment stimulates the receptors. It is also called data-driven processing. When a person looks at a woman named Mary, the sequence is light reflected from Mary, then an image on the retina, then signals in the optic nerve, then signals in the brain. In general the sequence is stimulus, receptors, neural signal and representation.",
"Top-down processing originates in the brain, at the top of the perceptual system. It is also called knowledge-driven processing. The perceiver brings knowledge, which is what you already know about Mary. The perceiver brings expectation, which is what you predict you are about to see. The perceiver brings attention, which decides which features are sampled.",
"Direct perception theories say that perception comes from the stimuli available in the environment. Gibson emphasized the optic array, which is the pattern of light available to a moving observer. It specifies the layout of the scene. Constructive perception theories say that people actively construct perceptions using expectations. Knowledge, experience and context do part of the work. Illusions show that what is perceived can depart from the stimulus.",
"The recognition-by-components theory of Irving Biederman (1987) says we perceive objects by first perceiving elementary three-dimensional features. These building blocks are called geons. About 36 geons are proposed. Three geons in the right arrangement identify most common objects. The same parts can form different objects when they are arranged differently."
],
"remember": "Bottom-up processing starts at the receptors. Top-down processing brings knowledge, expectation and attention."
