"answers": [
"Perception as inference is an answer to ambiguity. If the image is ambiguous, what is perceived has to be an inference about the most likely cause. Helmholtz’s unconscious inference and Bayesian inference describe how the inference is made.",
"Helmholtz’s theory of unconscious inference (around 1860) says that some perceptions result from unconscious assumptions we make about the environment. It is a top-down account. The likelihood principle says we perceive the world in the way that is most likely, given our past experience. The inference is unconscious and involuntary. Unconscious here means that we cannot inspect it. It is not meant in the Freudian sense.",
"A retinal image, labeled (a), could come from two sources. Source (b) is two overlapping rectangles. Source (c) is a rectangle and an oddly notched shape lined up exactly. Both sources fit the image. Almost everyone chooses (b).",
"Bayesian inference makes the likelihood principle quantitative. The prior probability is what you believed before the stimulus arrived. One prior is that light usually comes from above. The likelihood is how well each interpretation would explain the stimulus. The posterior combines the prior and the likelihood into an updated interpretation. The posterior is proportional to the prior times the likelihood.",
"The stimulus is a disc that is light at the top and dark at the bottom. The likelihood is ambiguous. A bump lit from above and a dent lit from below produce the identical image. The prior favors light from above, so the posterior favors seeing a bump. Turning the image upside down can turn bumps into dents."
],
"remember": "Helmholtz’s likelihood principle picks the most likely cause. In Bayesian inference, the posterior is proportional to the prior times the likelihood."
