"answers": [
"Perceptual organization is the process by which the visual field is divided into objects before they are identified. The image does not label which features belong together. The Gestalt laws of perceptual organization describe how the mind groups the field into units.",
"In Wundt’s structuralism, overall experience is built by combining basic elements called sensations. Perception is the adding up of sensations. Movement would be built by adding up snapshots of an object in different places over time. Muybridge’s sequence of still photographs shows this idea.",
"The Gestalt psychologists (around 1912) rejected the idea that perceptions are formed by adding up sensations. They said the mind groups patterns according to laws of perceptual organization. In apparent movement, movement is perceived even though nothing physically moves through the path that is seen.",
"Good continuation means that lines tend to be seen as following the smoothest path. Points that connect into straight or smoothly curving lines are seen as belonging together. A contour that disappears behind an object is seen as continuing behind it.",
"Prägnanz, also called simplicity or good figure, means that a pattern is organized so the result is as simple as possible. The Olympic symbol is seen as five overlapping circles. It is not seen as nine irregular closed shapes fitted together, although that also fits the lines.",
"Similarity means that similar things appear grouped together. In one panel all the dots are red. When the color varies, the display reorganizes into columns. The positions of the dots stay the same. Grouping can depend on color, shape, size, orientation and lightness.",
"In the beach scene, good continuation makes the horizon and waterline continue behind the person. Similarity groups sand with sand, water with water and sky with sky. Prägnanz favors three simple horizontal bands. Figure and ground separates one person from the background.",
"The Gestalt position is that the laws are built into the visual system. Experience plays a minor role, so the laws should be universal across cultures and present very early in life. An experience-based view is that smooth contours and similar surfaces really do tend to belong to one object. The laws may reflect regularities the system has learned."
],
"remember": "The Gestalt psychologists said the mind groups patterns by laws such as good continuation, Prägnanz and similarity."
