"The 100-car database held 42,300 hours of driving, about two million miles. It had 82 crashes and collisions, 761 near crashes, 8,295 incidents and 20,000 matched periods of normal baseline driving. A crash or collision is contact with another vehicle, a fixed object, a pedestrian, a pedalcyclist or an animal. A near crash is a conflict that requires a rapid, severe evasive maneuver. An incident requires a smaller evasive maneuver.",
"In the 100-car results, inattention to the forward roadway was a primary contributing factor in many crashes. 80 percent of crashes and 65 percent of near crashes involved at least one form of inattention within three seconds before the conflict began. 93 percent of lead-vehicle crashes and minor collisions involved looking away. In 86 percent of those, the headway was greater than 2.0 seconds. Headway is the time gap between vehicles.",
"Secondary tasks that are moderately to very complex, and driver drowsiness, carry the highest crash risk. The graphs compare crash, near-crash, incident and baseline periods on three measures. They are the time the eyes were off the forward roadway, the number of glances away, and the mean length of the longest glance away."
