"q": "What do sensitivity and response bias mean in signal detection, and how are d′ and C used?",
"a": "Signal detection separates two things. Sensitivity is the ability to tell a target from background noise. Response bias is the tendency to answer “present” or “absent.” Participants look for a slanted line hidden among random dots. A hit is correctly reporting the line. A miss is failing to report it. A false alarm is reporting a line that is absent. A correct rejection is correctly saying it is absent. d′ measures sensitivity by combining hits and false alarms. A value of zero means the participant cannot tell target-present displays from target-absent displays. Larger values mean better discrimination. log(alpha) is another measure of sensitivity. C measures response bias. Positive values are conservative, negative values are liberal, and values near zero are neutral. A conservative observer needs stronger evidence before saying “present.”",
