"q": "How does the method of constant stimuli measure the Müller-Lyer illusion?",
"a": "In the Müller-Lyer illusion, a line with outward wings looks longer than a line with inward wings, even when the two lines are equal. Psychophysics connects perceptual judgments with measurable properties of the stimulus. The method of constant stimuli presents preset lengths of a line without wings beside a standard line with outward wings. Participants judge whether each comparison looks bigger or smaller. The psychometric function plots how often the observer says “bigger” at each length. The point of subjective equality is where “bigger” and “smaller” are equally likely. The extra length needed to look equal measures the strength of the illusion.",
