"a": "Apparent motion is seeing movement between stationary stimuli that are flashed one after the other. The interstimulus interval (ISI) is the gap between the first stimulus turning off and the next one turning on. With a very short gap the dots look simultaneous. With a long gap they look like separate flashes. At an intermediate gap, one dot seems to travel to the other location. Participants adjust the gap until the movement looks strongest. Dots that are farther apart need a longer gap, which is consistent with Korte’s laws.",
