"q": "What is the Simon effect?",
"a": "The Simon effect is faster, more accurate responding when the location of the stimulus matches the location of the response, even though location is irrelevant. It concerns response selection, which is choosing the action. A red or green square appears left or right of fixation. Participants press a right-hand key for green and a left-hand key for red. A green square on the right is congruent. A green square on the left is incongruent, and the response is slower. The location of the square activates the left-hand response, which competes with the correct right-hand response.",
