"a": "Rioja, Cekic, Bavelier and Baumgartner (2023) examined media multitasking, which is using several media at once, and sustained attention, which is keeping focus over time. There were three samples, from MTurk, from Prolific and from a university. The Media Multitasking Index (MMI) measures how many media are typically used together, weighted by the time spent with each. In the GradCPT, city and mountain pictures gradually replace one another. Participants press for the frequent city pictures and withhold the response for the occasional mountains. Higher MMI scores were associated with more reported attention problems, impulsivity and sensation seeking, and with poorer task performance. The association was strongest in the MTurk sample. A shorter measure asks how often multitasking happens, and it related less strongly to task performance. Attention differences were linked to using media at the same time, and not to total media hours alone.",
