"q": "What is the neighborhood size effect, and why is it confounded with word length?",
"a": "An orthographic neighborhood is the set of words made by changing one letter of a word. “Cat” has neighbors such as “bat,” “cot” and “can.” A phonological neighborhood changes one speech sound. In ordered recall, words with larger neighborhoods are remembered better than comparable words with fewer neighbors. Short words tend to have more neighbors, so word length and neighborhood size are confounded. When neighborhood size is equated, the usual recall advantage for short words has been found to disappear.",
