"a": "Sperling examined what remains available after a briefly flashed array of letters disappears. In whole report, observers try to report every letter and manage only a few. In partial report, a cue after the array names one row. Observers can report most or all of that row, although they did not know which row would be asked for. More letters were available at first than whole report shows. The advantage of partial report declines as the cue is delayed over about one second. Sensory memory briefly holds a rich trace that fades quickly.",
