"q": "What did the rats learn first in Tolman’s (1938) maze?",
"a": "Behaviorism dominated American psychology for decades, but some researchers were not strict behaviorists. Tolman (1938) trained rats to find food in a four-armed maze. First the rat explored the maze. Then it was placed in arm A with food in arm B, and it learned to turn right to get the food. When the rat was placed in arm C, it turned left and reached the same food.",
"score": 9,
"tier": "high",
"note": "Outside the slides, the maze experiment is usually cited as Tolman and colleagues (1946), and the cognitive map paper as Tolman (1948). The slides date the maze to 1938.",
