"q": "How did early digital computers shape the information-processing approach?",
"a": "Early digital computers in the 1950s processed information in a sequence of stages. One example sequence is input, input processor, memory unit, arithmetic unit and output. The information-processing approach used this idea to build testable models of the mind. Two questions followed. How much information can the mind absorb? How does attention select some information and ignore the rest?",
