"q": "Why does perception feel automatic, and what does machine perception show?",
"a": "Perception is fast and effortless, so it feels automatic. The processing behind it is largely invisible to us. Roughly half the primate cortex is devoted to vision alone. Machine perception has repeatedly fallen short. Machines handled chess, proofs and algebra decades before reliable object recognition. The DARPA Urban Challenge (2007) showed how hard it is for robot vehicles to interpret a changing environment.",
