"q": "How do top-down influences change pain, as in Atlas and colleagues (2012)?",
"a": "The direct pathway model is an early account of pain. In it, nociceptors in the skin send pain messages straight to the brain. Nociceptors are receptors that respond to damaging stimulation. A purely bottom-up account predicts that pain intensity should follow tissue damage. Expectation, attention and distraction all change how much pain is experienced. In Atlas and colleagues (2012), participants could see pain medication being delivered. Activity increased in frontal regions and decreased in areas associated with nociception.",
