"q": "What is Helmholtz’s theory of unconscious inference, and what is the likelihood principle?",
"a": "Helmholtz’s theory of unconscious inference (around 1860) says that some perceptions result from unconscious assumptions we make about the environment. It is a top-down account. The likelihood principle says we perceive the world in the way that is most likely, given our past experience. The inference is unconscious and involuntary. Unconscious here means that we cannot inspect it. It is not meant in the Freudian sense.",
