"q": "What are the prior, the likelihood and the posterior in Bayesian inference?",
"a": "Bayesian inference makes the likelihood principle quantitative. The prior probability is what you believed before the stimulus arrived. One prior is that light usually comes from above. The likelihood is how well each interpretation would explain the stimulus. The posterior combines the prior and the likelihood into an updated interpretation. The posterior is proportional to the prior times the likelihood.",
