"q": "What evidence goes against a strict early filter?",
"a": "A strict filter has a problem, which is that participants’ names get through. In Moray (1959), about one third of listeners detected their own name in the unattended ear. Participants can also shadow meaningful messages that switch ears. This was shown in the “Dear Aunt Jane” experiment of Gray and Wedderburn (1960). The left ear received “Dear, 4, Jane.” The shadowed right ear received “8, Aunt, 2.” Participants reported “Dear Aunt Jane.”",
